"{\n \"url\": \"https://vine-mandevilla-7ed.notion.site/Inventory-System-Breakdown-3b37216d8bc580a889c7cad05c83d401\",\n \"text\": \"Skip to content\\nPray For Plagues, Souls-borne Prototype\\n/\\nSystems Documentation\\n/\\nInventory System Breakdown\\nGet Notion free\\nInventory System Breakdown\\n4 more properties\\nOverview\\nThe inventory system is built as a data‑driven, modular framework that manages all player‑owned items, including weapons, consumables, and inspectable objects. It serves as the central hub for item acquisition, storage, and equipment management, ensuring that world interactions and combat systems remain tightly connected through a unified data pipeline.\\nAt its core, the system uses a structured item database where each item is defined through a UDataTable entry. This allows every item—whether a simple pickup or a fully featured weapon—to share a consistent data format while still supporting specialized behavior. When the player interacts with an item in the world, the interaction system passes the item’s data directly into the inventory, ensuring seamless integration between world objects and player progression.\\nEach Item loaded asynchronously (Unless loaded during the level load) during gameplay to smooth the experience and to avoid lag and interruptions.\\nDisclaimer: All UI widgets featured in the GIF’s are not final and only meant to showcase the system and its mechanics\\nItem Data Table Structure\\nA small detail on how the items are managed in the Data Table.\\nItems summary and items count\\nIndividual item’s detail. Weapons have special properties detailed in the combat section\\nInventory\\nThe inventory is divided into 4 categories: Consumables, Craftables, Weapons & Key Items. Each Inventory section has its own item entry stack count except for key items. \\nCategory Showcase\\nSome items are stackable, like consumables while others such as weapons are non stackable\\nStack Overflow Handling\\nIf an Item exceeds the inventories capacity or the items stack, a message will show up notifying the player that the item is sent to the stash.\\nAction Context Pop Up Menu\\nEach Item has its own action context menu. Weapons can be equipped and unequipped while consumables can be used. Key items such as Keys used automatically when interacting with the object that requires that item. Items can be dropped but cannot be recovered\\nThe items pop up menu specifically with weapons, currently is not associated with its actual stats pulled from the Curve Table. Later down the line it will be fixed.\\nKey Items Usage Handling\\nOnce Key Item is used and has no further use, the inventory will get rid of it automatically\\nWeapon Equip/Unequip Handling\\nAs was showcased in the Combat Breakdown section, weapons receive special handling within the inventory. When a weapon is equipped, the system applies all of its associated data to the player, including its animation layer, damage scaling, special abilities, mapping context, and custom montages. This makes equipping a weapon a full character transformation rather than a simple stat change, and ensures that combat behavior updates instantly and consistently.\\nOverall, the inventory system acts as the connective tissue between exploration, interaction, and combat. By keeping item definitions centralized, modular, and data‑driven, the system makes adding new items fast, safe, and scalable while ensuring that equipping or using an item has immediate and meaningful gameplay impact.\\n\\n\\nShare site\\nInventory System Breakdo…\\nGet Notion free\\nInventory System Breakdo…\\nOverview\\nItem Data Table Structure\\nInventory\\nCopy URL\\nSign up or log in\\nReport page\\nCookie settings\\nStrictly necessary\\nEssential for the site to function. Always On.\\nFunctional\\nUsed to remember preference selections and provide enhanced features.\\nAnalytics\\nUsed to measure usage and improve your experience.\\nMarketing\\nUsed for targeted advertising.\",\n \"media\